# <%= current_page.data.title %>

Use this page as a reference when you first meet an LAA Java service. It explains what the
microservice template provides and where to look; it is not a second build tutorial.

To create and evolve a service yourself, follow the
[library service tutorials](java-training.html). This page stays focused on navigating the template.

## What the template gives you

The [LAA Spring Boot microservice template](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/laa-spring-boot-microservice-template)
starts with two Gradle modules:

| Module | Responsibility |
| --- | --- |
| `spring-boot-microservice-api` | Owns the OpenAPI contract and generated Java interfaces and models. |
| `spring-boot-microservice-service` | Owns the runnable Spring Boot application and its implementation. |

The root build coordinates both modules. The service module applies the shared LAA Spring Boot
Gradle plugin, which supplies common build, test, quality, and dependency-management conventions.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A[OpenAPI contract] --> B[Generated API module]
    B --> C[Controller]
    C --> D[Service]
    D --> E[Repository]
    E --> F[Database]
```

## A five-minute orientation

From a fresh checkout:

```bash
./gradlew projects
./gradlew build
./gradlew :spring-boot-microservice-service:bootRun
```

Then inspect these files in order:

1. `settings.gradle` - included modules and plugin repositories.
2. `spring-boot-microservice-api/open-api-specification.yml` - the HTTP contract.
3. `spring-boot-microservice-service/build.gradle` - service dependencies and build configuration.
4. `spring-boot-microservice-service/src/main/resources/application.yml` - runtime defaults.
5. The service module's `controller`, `service`, `mapper`, `entity`, and `repository` packages - the
   request-to-database path.
6. `src/test` and `src/integrationTest` - fast isolated tests and tests that cross infrastructure
   boundaries.

This is enough to locate a change before editing it. Generated files under the API module's
`generated` directory are build output: change the OpenAPI document and regenerate them instead of
editing them directly.

## Where changes belong

| If you need to change… | Start with… | Then verify… |
| --- | --- | --- |
| An endpoint or request/response shape | `open-api-specification.yml` | Generated interfaces/models and controller compilation |
| Business behaviour | A service class | Focused unit tests and HTTP behaviour |
| HTTP status or error representation | Controller advice and the OpenAPI error schema | MVC or integration tests |
| Mapping between API and persistence models | A MapStruct mapper | Mapper tests and generated mapping warnings |
| Stored data | Entity, repository, and a migration | Repository tests against the production database type |
| Runtime configuration | `application.yml` and environment variables | Local profile and deployment configuration |
| Build conventions or dependency versions | Shared plugin/BOM usage first | `./gradlew build` and dependency insight |

Avoid putting business rules in controllers, exposing JPA entities as API models, editing generated
sources, or relying on Hibernate to mutate production schemas.

## Template defaults are a starting point

The template may contain demonstration choices such as H2, `schema.sql`, seed data, example item
endpoints, or permissive local tooling. Treat these as scaffolding, not production recommendations.
A real service should make its own evidenced decisions about:

- PostgreSQL or another production datastore and versioned Flyway migrations
- authentication and authorisation using supported LAA starters
- secrets and environment-specific configuration
- observability, health checks, and operational ownership
- deployment, rollback, data migration, and security controls

The tutorials replace the demonstration persistence with PostgreSQL and Flyway and show how the
contract, implementation, and test seams evolve together.

